# Lower residential street, doorstep of Dwelling No. 2.
# Mrs. Peake: fisherman's wife, salt mender; neighbor to the boardinghouse and quay.
NPC: salt_mender
LOCATION: lower_residential
SCHEDULE: morning=lower_house_2, midday=quay, evening=lower_house_2, night=lower_house_2

TOPIC: default
  GATE: NOT topic_done(salt_mender, default) AND coat = plain
  TAG: mender_plain
  MRS. PEAKE: "Watch where you step on that brine net. It's drying in the sun."
  [She uses a bone netting needle to knot tarred cord with practiced, rhythmic snaps.]
  MRS. PEAKE: "You've got the look of someone with a room that gets cold by midnight.\nIf you're looking for Mrs. Almy's, it's three doors up the hill."

TOPIC: default
  GATE: NOT topic_done(salt_mender, default)
  TAG: mender_badge
  MRS. PEAKE: "Nothing stolen on this alley, Officer.\nWe don't keep silver spoons to tempt anybody."
  WALTER CORWIN: "I'm not looking for thieves, Mrs. Peake."
  MRS. PEAKE: "Then you're looking for trouble.\nDown here, the two usually arrive in the same coat."

TOPIC: night_sounds
  GATE: always
  TAG: mender_sounds
  LABEL: "Ask about unusual sounds from the harbor at night"
  TIME: 7
  WALTER CORWIN: "Do you hear the water from this porch?"
  MRS. PEAKE: "I've heard this harbor since eighteen eighty, Walter Corwin.\nI know what the swell sounds like against the breakwater."
  WALTER CORWIN: "And lately?"
  MRS. PEAKE: "Three nights back, just before the bells for three. The tide went dead flat.\nNo chop. No lap against the piles. Like grease on a skillet."
  WALTER CORWIN: "And then?"
  MRS. PEAKE: "A dragging sound. Heavy. Not a keel scraping mud.\nBeneath the pilings. Like something enormous being shifted through wet sand without a wave to move it.\nMy husband took his boots off and sat by the stove until daylight."
  NOTEBOOK: mender_tide_silence | "Mrs. Peake: dead flat tide at 3:00 AM before the deaths, followed by an unnatural heavy dragging through silt beneath the harbor piles."
  EVIDENCE: sub_harbor_drag

TOPIC: naomi_and_the_boy
  GATE: evidence(naomi)
  TAG: mender_naomi
  LABEL: "Ask if she spoke to Naomi Freeman"
  TIME: 5
  WALTER CORWIN: "Did Naomi come past your porch?"
  MRS. PEAKE: "She brought me a tin of lard from town. Said the boy had torn his trouser knee on the rocks and asked if I had strong sailmaker's thread."
  WALTER CORWIN: "Did she say why they were by the rocks?"
  MRS. PEAKE: "Looking at the island. The boy asked if people lived out there.\nShe told him people used to work there, but the town had taken the timber away."
  [She bites through the heavy twine with her teeth.]
  MRS. PEAKE: "I told her nobody went out to that mud for sixty years.\nShe looked at me and said, 'The mud didn't make the debt, ma'am. Men did.'\nI couldn't find an argument to give her back."
  NOTEBOOK: mender_naomi_quote | "Naomi Freeman on the drowned island: 'The mud didn't make the debt, ma'am. Men did.'"
  EVIDENCE: naomi_moral_clarity

TOPIC: crew_omission
  GATE: topic_done(local_historian, crew_omission_official) AND NOT topic_done(salt_mender, crew_omission)
  TAG: salt_mender_crew_omission
  LABEL: "Ask why the town's history leaves out the crew's names"
  TIME: 5
  WALTER CORWIN: "The men who drowned in 1823. Why aren't their names anywhere?"
  [Mrs. Peake does not look up from the net.]
  MRS. PEAKE: "They are."
  WALTER CORWIN: "Where?"
  MRS. PEAKE: "Down here.\nEvery family on this alley has a name from that water.\nWe don't need a committee to spell it for us."
  WALTER CORWIN: "I mean in the record. In the memorial. In the school reader."
  MRS. PEAKE: "I know what you mean."
  [She bites through the twine and begins the next row.]
  MRS. PEAKE: "The hill decides what goes in the reader.\nWe decided a long time ago that it wasn't worth the argument.\nNaomi Freeman hadn't made that decision yet.\nYou can see how that went for her."
  NOTEBOOK: peake_crew_omission | "Mrs. Peake says the drowned crew's names are preserved in the lower harbor families, not in any official record. The hill's version was not worth fighting. Naomi Freeman had not accepted that. Mrs. Peake did not say this as an accusation."